Building the Debian package with sbuild and Podman
==================================================

Build the reusable Debian 13 sbuild image from the repository root:

    podman build --tag localhost/xymon-debian13-sbuild -f Containerfile .

Run an isolated binary package build:

    artifacts="$(dirname "$PWD")/xymon-sbuild-artifacts"
    mkdir -p "$artifacts"
    podman run --rm \
        --cap-add=SYS_ADMIN \
        --pid=private \
        --security-opt label=disable \
        --volume "$PWD:/source:ro" \
        --volume "$artifacts:/artifacts" \
        localhost/xymon-debian13-sbuild

The container creates a Debian source package and builds it with sbuild in an
ephemeral Debian 13 (trixie) buildd chroot. Build dependencies are installed
fresh from the Debian repository for each build. Binary and source packages,
the .buildinfo and .changes files, and the complete sbuild log persist in the
sibling xymon-sbuild-artifacts directory. List them after the build with:

    ls -lh "$artifacts"

The source checkout is mounted read-only. SYS_ADMIN is required only for
schroot's temporary mounts; the container is not privileged and receives no
host block devices. The chroot gets a private, restricted proc filesystem and
only the full, null, random, tty, urandom, and zero pseudo-devices. It does not
mount sysfs or the complete /dev tree.

Rebuild the image periodically to refresh its base chroot. sbuild updates that
chroot and resolves the package's current build dependencies before each build.

GitHub Actions
--------------

The Sync master from Salsa workflow is started manually. It creates the GitHub
`master` branch from Debian Salsa on its first run and only fast-forwards it
afterward. The workflow fails without changing the branch if the GitHub and
Salsa histories diverge.

The Build Debian packages workflow runs the same Containerfile and
xymon-testbuild entry point on GitHub-hosted Ubuntu runners. It uses Docker on
the ephemeral runner in privileged mode for schroot compatibility and uploads
all packages, source artifacts, build records, and the sbuild log. Build records
are uploaded even when sbuild fails. The restricted Podman invocation above is
still recommended for persistent development hosts.

The Release Debian packages workflow is started manually from the repository's
default branch. It rebuilds the package, creates an `axians/<debian-version>`
GitHub release, and attaches the packages, source artifacts, build records,
sbuild log, and SHA256SUMS. Release assets and tags are not signed.
